      "content": {
        "text": "Let's use a useful pairing from the chapter.\n\n(3 + 2√2)(3 - 2√2)\n= 9 - 8\n= 1.\n\nSo 3 - 2√2 is the reciprocal of 3 + 2√2. This turns difficult-looking 1/x expressions into simple surd arithmetic.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use the difference of squares:\n\n(a+b√c)(a-b√c)=a^2-b^2c.\n\nFor 2±√3, the product is 4-3=1.\nTherefore 1/(2+√3)=2-√3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
